# Native cross-module moves against the flatten oracle: same request, both ways.
#
# opt_retime_hier.ys asserts what the native crossing builds, cell by cell, and
# proves it. opt_retime_hier_oracle.ys does the same for flatten-then-retime.
# Each is right about itself and neither says anything about the other. This
# file runs one request through both and compares the answers, which is the only
# way to tell a crossing that is correct from a crossing that is correct and
# also the move that was asked for.
#
# The comparison is between the two *results*, not between each result and the
# original. Both paths already prove against the original in their own files, so
# proving that again here would pass on two implementations that had drifted
# into making different legal moves. gold is the native netlist, flattened after
# the fact; gate is the flat netlist the oracle produced. Same ports, so the
# miter is the ordinary one.
#
# The miter on its own would not be enough, and it is worth being precise about
# why. Any two correct retimings of one design are equivalent to each other -
# that is what makes retiming retiming - so a proof that the two results agree
# says both paths made *a* correct move, never that they made the *same* one.
# Deleting the oracle's opt_retime call entirely still passes the miter in the
# first section, which was checked rather than argued.
#
# So each section carries three claims and needs all three. The flop count and
# the cell census say the two results are the same size. Two selections say the
# register is in the same place: which cell drives its D and which reads its Q,
# named by cell rather than by net, because flattening gives the combinational
# cells the same names on both paths and only the flop's own name differs. And
# the miter says the place they agree on is the right one. Dropping the oracle's
# move, or pointing it at a different cut, fails the position assertions; a
# wrong netlist fails the miter.
#
# Hop counts in those selections are not uniform, and the reason is worth
# knowing: flatten leaves an instance-port net as an alias of the parent net
# rather than merging the two, so a register whose D or Q crossed a boundary
# sits one hop further back than one that did not. The depths are commented
# where they differ.
#
# "The same request" needs one qualification. A request is a flop and a cut
# cell, and flattening renames cells: inv_d inside hier_dst becomes u_dst.inv_d
# in top. So the two paths spell the same cut differently and there is no way
# around that. Every section below names the same physical cell twice.
#
# What the harness found, stated up front because the refusal sections are the
# reason this file is worth having:
#
#   - Where the oracle can express the request and the native pass accepts it,
#     the two agree, and not merely up to equivalence: same flop count, same
#     retiming, proved. Seven shapes below.
#   - The oracle cannot express a pure crossing at all - a move whose cut is the
#     instance, which relocates the register and touches no logic. Flattening
#     deletes the boundary, and with it the only thing that move does. Asking
#     anyway is a hard error rather than a refusal; the last section pins it.
#   - The oracle never refuses a cross-module move. All seven cross-module
#     refusals the pass can make and the oracle can be asked about - which is
#     every one in opt_retime_hier_refusal.ys except the blackbox, where there
#     is nothing to flatten - are cases the oracle accepts, and the result it
#     produces is correct; each one is proved below. So the oracle is an oracle
#     for acceptance and for what the answer should be, and is worthless as an
#     oracle for refusal. It answers "is there a retiming here", and the pass is
#     answering "is there a retiming here that survives being written back as
#     hierarchical RTL", which is a strictly smaller question.
#
# That last point is the one to carry forward. A driver must not treat a
# flatten-then-retime result as evidence that the native pass is being too
# conservative, because for four of the seven refusals it is not conservatism at
# all - it is hierarchy holding a constraint that flattening threw away.
#
# Three of those four carry no marker at all, which by the convention means the
# answer is no rather than not yet, and all three turn out to have a move: a
# register can cross into a module with no clock as long as it stops in front of
# the instance instead of inside it; a gated clock the parent cannot see stops
# mattering once the logic moves instead of the register; and the init a landing
# net already carries can be inert. None of that changes what the pass should do
# - the refusals are right about the form it produces - but a driver sorting
# candidates by marker reads an unmarked refusal as the end of the search, and
# for these three it is not.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Part 1: requests both paths can express.
#
# Seven shapes: the four ways a crossing can go, plus the three things that can
# happen on top of one - two boundaries in a row, a resize on landing, and a
# reader left behind on the vacated net.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shape the handoff note calls known-good, now asserted as agreement rather
# than as two files that happen to match.
log -header "Agree: forward into a child, cut inside it"
log -push
design -reset
read_verilog -icells opt_retime_hier.v
hierarchy -top top
check -assert
design -save orig

opt_retime -flop f1 -cut inv_d -forward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_from top
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_to hier_dst
check -assert
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff
flatten
hierarchy -top top
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff
select -assert-count 4 c:* t:$scopeinfo %d
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %ci2 c:u_dst.inv_d %i
select -assert-count 0 t:$dff %ci2 c:u_src.add_s %i
design -save native

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u_dst
select -clear
opt_retime -flop f1 -cut u_dst.inv_d -forward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
# Both ends are top once the boundary is gone, which is the oracle saying it
# cannot see that a boundary was crossed rather than saying none was.
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_from top
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_to top
check -assert
flatten
hierarchy -top top
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff
select -assert-count 4 c:* t:$scopeinfo %d
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %ci2 c:u_dst.inv_d %i
select -assert-count 0 t:$dff %ci2 c:u_src.add_s %i
rename top gate
design -import native -as gold
miter -equiv -flatten -make_assert gold gate miter
hierarchy -top miter
proc; opt
sat -seq 8 -prove-asserts -verify
design -reset
log -pop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backward is where a flop-count assertion would have been wrong if it had been
# written as an invariant: the adder has a second live operand and the move
# clones onto it. Two registers on both sides is the claim - the two paths agree
# about the clone as well as about the mover.
log -header "Agree: backward into a child, cut inside it"
log -push
design -reset
read_verilog -icells opt_retime_hier.v
hierarchy -top top
check -assert
design -save orig

opt_retime -flop f1 -cut add_s -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_from top
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_to hier_src
check -assert
select -assert-count 2 t:$dff
select -assert-count 2 hier_src/t:$dff
flatten
hierarchy -top top
select -assert-count 2 t:$dff
select -assert-count 5 c:* t:$scopeinfo %d
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %co2 c:u_src.add_s %i
# one hop further than the others: the register's D is an instance-port net,
# and flatten leaves those as an alias of the parent net rather than merging
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %ci3 c:xor_t %i
design -save native

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u_src
select -clear
opt_retime -flop f1 -cut u_src.add_s -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
check -assert
flatten
hierarchy -top top
select -assert-count 2 t:$dff
select -assert-count 5 c:* t:$scopeinfo %d
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %co2 c:u_src.add_s %i
# one hop further than the others: the register's D is an instance-port net,
# and flatten leaves those as an alias of the parent net rather than merging
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %ci3 c:xor_t %i
rename top gate
design -import native -as gold
miter -equiv -flatten -make_assert gold gate miter
hierarchy -top miter
proc; opt
sat -seq 8 -prove-asserts -verify
design -reset
log -pop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two shapes that leave a child rather than enter one. kid holds a register
# at each of its ports and top holds a cell on each side of it: p1 drives the
# input, t2 reads the output. So fb has somewhere to go backward that is outside
# kid, and ff has somewhere to go forward that is outside kid, and both cuts are
# cells the oracle can name after flattening.
#
# One fixture, two sections, because the design is the same and only the request
# differs - which is the point a differential file is making anyway.
log -header "Agree: backward out of a child, cut in the parent"
log -push
design -reset
read_verilog -icells <<EOF
module kid (clk, x, y, zin, zout);
  input clk;
  input [7:0] x, zin;
  output [7:0] y, zout;
  (* init = 8'h11 *) wire [7:0] xq;
  wire [7:0] zq;
  $dff #(.WIDTH(8), .CLK_POLARITY(1'b1)) fb (.CLK(clk), .D(x), .Q(xq));
  $not #(.A_WIDTH(8), .Y_WIDTH(8), .A_SIGNED(0)) k_not (.A(xq), .Y(y));
  $xor #(.A_WIDTH(8), .B_WIDTH(8), .Y_WIDTH(8), .A_SIGNED(0), .B_SIGNED(0))
    k_xor (.A(zin), .B(8'h0f), .Y(zq));
  $dff #(.WIDTH(8), .CLK_POLARITY(1'b1)) ff (.CLK(clk), .D(zq), .Q(zout));
endmodule
module top (clk, a, b, o1, o2);
  input clk;
  input [7:0] a, b;
  output [7:0] o1, o2;
  wire [7:0] pre, y, zout;
  $not #(.A_WIDTH(8), .Y_WIDTH(8), .A_SIGNED(0)) p1 (.A(a), .Y(pre));
  kid u (.clk(clk), .x(pre), .y(y), .zin(b), .zout(zout));
  $buf #(.WIDTH(8)) t1 (.A(y), .Y(o1));
  $not #(.A_WIDTH(8), .Y_WIDTH(8), .A_SIGNED(0)) t2 (.A(zout), .Y(o2));
endmodule
EOF
hierarchy -top top
# ff's Q is the output port itself, and a port wire is not somewhere Verilog
# lets an attribute be written.
setattr -set init 8'h22 kid/w:zout
select -clear
check -assert
design -save orig

opt_retime -flop fb -cut p1 -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_from kid
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_to top
check -assert
select -assert-count 2 t:$dff
select -assert-count 1 top/t:$dff
flatten
hierarchy -top top
select -assert-count 2 t:$dff
select -assert-count 7 c:* t:$scopeinfo %d
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %co2 c:p1 %i
select -assert-count 0 t:$dff %ci2 c:p1 %i
design -save native

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u
select -clear
opt_retime -flop u.fb -cut p1 -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
check -assert
flatten
hierarchy -top top
select -assert-count 2 t:$dff
select -assert-count 7 c:* t:$scopeinfo %d
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %co2 c:p1 %i
select -assert-count 0 t:$dff %ci2 c:p1 %i
rename top gate
design -import native -as gold
miter -equiv -flatten -make_assert gold gate miter
hierarchy -top miter
proc; opt
sat -seq 8 -prove-asserts -verify
log -pop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
log -header "Agree: forward out of a child, cut in the parent"
log -push
design -load orig
opt_retime -flop ff -cut t2 -forward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_from kid
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_to top
check -assert
select -assert-count 2 t:$dff
select -assert-count 1 top/t:$dff
flatten
hierarchy -top top
select -assert-count 2 t:$dff
select -assert-count 7 c:* t:$scopeinfo %d
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %ci2 c:t2 %i
select -assert-count 0 t:$dff %co2 c:t2 %i
design -save native

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u
select -clear
opt_retime -flop u.ff -cut t2 -forward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
check -assert
flatten
hierarchy -top top
select -assert-count 2 t:$dff
select -assert-count 7 c:* t:$scopeinfo %d
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %ci2 c:t2 %i
select -assert-count 0 t:$dff %co2 c:t2 %i
rename top gate
design -import native -as gold
miter -equiv -flatten -make_assert gold gate miter
hierarchy -top miter
proc; opt
sat -seq 8 -prove-asserts -verify
design -reset
log -pop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two boundaries, which the oracle reaches by inlining two instances rather than
# one. Worth its own section because the native path crosses twice and the
# oracle crosses zero times, so agreement here is agreement about a composition
# and not just about a step.
log -header "Agree: sibling to sibling, two boundaries"
log -push
design -reset
read_verilog -icells <<EOF
module sib_a (clk, x, y);
  input clk;
  input [7:0] x;
  output [7:0] y;
  wire [7:0] t;
  $add #(.A_WIDTH(8), .B_WIDTH(8), .Y_WIDTH(8), .A_SIGNED(0), .B_SIGNED(0))
    a_add (.A(x), .B(8'h03), .Y(t));
  $dff #(.WIDTH(8), .CLK_POLARITY(1'b1)) fa (.CLK(clk), .D(t), .Q(y));
endmodule
module sib_b (clk, p, q);
  input clk;
  input [7:0] p;
  output [7:0] q;
  $not #(.A_WIDTH(8), .Y_WIDTH(8), .A_SIGNED(0)) b_not (.A(p), .Y(q));
endmodule
module top (clk, a, out);
  input clk;
  input [7:0] a;
  output [7:0] out;
  wire [7:0] m;
  sib_a u_a (.clk(clk), .x(a), .y(m));
  sib_b u_b (.clk(clk), .p(m), .q(out));
endmodule
EOF
hierarchy -top top
setattr -set init 8'h00 sib_a/w:y
select -clear
check -assert
design -save orig

opt_retime -flop fa -cut b_not -forward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_from sib_a
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_to sib_b
check -assert
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff
flatten
hierarchy -top top
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff
select -assert-count 3 c:* t:$scopeinfo %d
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %ci2 c:u_b.b_not %i
select -assert-count 0 t:$dff %co2 c:u_b.b_not %i
design -save native

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u_a top/c:u_b
select -clear
opt_retime -flop u_a.fa -cut u_b.b_not -forward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
check -assert
flatten
hierarchy -top top
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff
select -assert-count 3 c:* t:$scopeinfo %d
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %ci2 c:u_b.b_not %i
select -assert-count 0 t:$dff %co2 c:u_b.b_not %i
rename top gate
design -import native -as gold
miter -equiv -flatten -make_assert gold gate miter
hierarchy -top miter
proc; opt
sat -seq 8 -prove-asserts -verify
design -reset
log -pop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The crossing moves eight bits through an eight-bit port and the move after it
# narrows them to the one bit that picks between the mux's constant operands.
# The oracle does the narrowing with no port involved at all, so agreement here
# says the width the crossing chose did not survive into the answer, which is
# what it should not do.
log -header "Agree: a crossing followed by a resize onto a mux select"
log -push
design -reset
read_verilog -icells <<EOF
module sel_kid (clk, s, y);
  input clk, s;
  output [7:0] y;
  $mux #(.WIDTH(8)) m0 (.A(8'h00), .B(8'hff), .S(s), .Y(y));
endmodule
module top (clk, sin, out);
  input clk, sin;
  output [7:0] out;
  wire [7:0] mid;
  (* init = 8'hff *) wire [7:0] midq;
  sel_kid u (.clk(clk), .s(sin), .y(mid));
  $dff #(.WIDTH(8), .CLK_POLARITY(1'b1)) f1 (.CLK(clk), .D(mid), .Q(midq));
  $not #(.A_WIDTH(8), .Y_WIDTH(8), .A_SIGNED(0)) o1 (.A(midq), .Y(out));
endmodule
EOF
hierarchy -top top
check -assert
design -save orig

opt_retime -flop f1 -cut m0 -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_to sel_kid
check -assert
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff
# one bit, in the child
select -assert-count 1 sel_kid/c:f1 %x:+[D] sel_kid/w:s %i
flatten
hierarchy -top top
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff
select -assert-count 3 c:* t:$scopeinfo %d
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %co2 c:u.m0 %i
select -assert-count 0 t:$dff %ci2 c:u.m0 %i
design -save native

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u
select -clear
opt_retime -flop f1 -cut u.m0 -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
check -assert
flatten
hierarchy -top top
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff
select -assert-count 3 c:* t:$scopeinfo %d
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %co2 c:u.m0 %i
select -assert-count 0 t:$dff %ci2 c:u.m0 %i
rename top gate
design -import native -as gold
miter -equiv -flatten -make_assert gold gate miter
hierarchy -top miter
proc; opt
sat -seq 8 -prove-asserts -verify
design -reset
log -pop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A second reader of the vacated net. The two paths solve it differently and
# have to agree anyway: natively kid gains an output port carrying the old
# value, and in the flat netlist there is no port to gain, so the register that
# feeds the reader is simply a second register. Same circuit, one of them with a
# boundary through the middle of it.
log -header "Agree: a reader left on the vacated net"
log -push
design -reset
read_verilog -icells <<EOF
module kid (clk, x, z);
  input clk;
  input [7:0] x;
  output [7:0] z;
  $not #(.A_WIDTH(8), .Y_WIDTH(8), .A_SIGNED(0)) k_not (.A(x), .Y(z));
endmodule
module top (clk, a, out1, out2);
  input clk;
  input [7:0] a;
  output [7:0] out1, out2;
  (* init = 8'h00 *) wire [7:0] midq;
  $dff #(.WIDTH(8), .CLK_POLARITY(1'b1)) f1 (.CLK(clk), .D(a), .Q(midq));
  kid u (.clk(clk), .x(midq), .z(out1));
  $not #(.A_WIDTH(8), .Y_WIDTH(8), .A_SIGNED(0)) tap (.A(midq), .Y(out2));
endmodule
EOF
hierarchy -top top
check -assert
design -save orig

opt_retime -flop f1 -cut k_not -forward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_to kid
check -assert
# the mover plus one for the reader the crossing would have stranded
select -assert-count 2 t:$dff
select -assert-count 2 kid/t:$dff
select -assert-count 1 kid/w:x_reg
flatten
hierarchy -top top
select -assert-count 2 t:$dff
select -assert-count 4 c:* t:$scopeinfo %d
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %ci2 c:u.k_not %i
select -assert-count 0 t:$dff %co2 c:u.k_not %i
design -save native

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u
select -clear
opt_retime -flop f1 -cut u.k_not -forward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
check -assert
flatten
hierarchy -top top
select -assert-count 2 t:$dff
select -assert-count 4 c:* t:$scopeinfo %d
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff %ci2 c:u.k_not %i
select -assert-count 0 t:$dff %co2 c:u.k_not %i
rename top gate
design -import native -as gold
miter -equiv -flatten -make_assert gold gate miter
hierarchy -top miter
proc; opt
sat -seq 8 -prove-asserts -verify
design -reset
log -pop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Part 2: where the two answers differ.
#
# Every section here is the oracle accepting a request the pass refuses, and
# every one of them proves the oracle's netlist correct before saying so - the
# finding is "there is a correct move here and the pass does not make it", which
# is a different and much stronger statement than "the two disagree".
#
# They are not all the same kind of disagreement, and the difference is what a
# driver has to act on:
#
#   (a) the pass could make this move and does not - a gap, marked (unsupported)
#   (b) the move exists, and the refusal is about the form the pass would
#       produce rather than about the move: relocating the register is
#       impossible or unsafe, keeping it where it is and moving the logic across
#       instead is neither, and this pass only moves registers
#   (c) the oracle is the one that is wrong - its netlist is correct and cannot
#       be written back as hierarchical RTL at all
#
# Three of each of (a) and (b), and one (c). Together with the blackbox in
# Part 3 that is every refusal in opt_retime_hier_refusal.ys.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (a) The known gap. The path leaves top through u_src and comes back, so the
# flop and the cut are in the same module with a boundary in between. Crossing
# is not what is missing - the walk that finds the path is one module wide, and
# the register would have to cross in, move, and cross back out.
#
# The oracle inlines u_src, the path stops leaving anything, and today's pass
# makes the move. So the refusal costs a real move, and the marker says so.
log -header "Differ: the path leaves the module and comes back"
log -push
design -reset
read_verilog -icells opt_retime_hier.v
hierarchy -top top
check -assert
design -save orig

opt_retime -flop f1 -cut xor_t -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved false
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.refusal "The before-path of flop f1 leaves top at port y of instance u_src, and opt_retime follows a chain within one module (unsupported)."
scratchpad -assert-unset opt.did_something

design -load orig
flatten
hierarchy -top top
design -save goldflat

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u_src
select -clear
opt_retime -flop f1 -cut xor_t -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
check -assert
# the mover and a clone at each of the two cells it passed
select -assert-count 3 t:$dff
flatten
hierarchy -top top
rename top gate
design -import goldflat -as gold
miter -equiv -flatten -make_assert gold gate miter
hierarchy -top miter
proc; opt
sat -seq 8 -prove-asserts -verify
design -reset
log -pop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (a) again, and the more surprising one. A cell reading the vacated net gets a
# port punched for it; a module output does not, because handing it the old
# value leaves top's latency unchanged only if something outside top
# compensates, and nothing here knows whether anything does.
#
# The oracle does not have that problem and does not need the reasoning: it
# duplicates the cut cell, so the output keeps its own combinational copy and
# the register moves past the other one. That technique would work across a
# boundary too - duplicate k_not inside kid and punch a port for the copy - so
# this is a gap and not a necessity, which is what (unsupported) claims.
log -header "Differ: the vacated net drives a module output"
log -push
design -reset
read_verilog -icells <<EOF
module kid (clk, x, z);
  input clk;
  input [7:0] x;
  output [7:0] z;
  $not #(.A_WIDTH(8), .Y_WIDTH(8), .A_SIGNED(0)) k_not (.A(x), .Y(z));
endmodule
module top (clk, a, out1, out2);
  input clk;
  input [7:0] a;
  output [7:0] out1, out2;
  kid u (.clk(clk), .x(a), .z(out2));
  $dff #(.WIDTH(8), .CLK_POLARITY(1'b1)) f1 (.CLK(clk), .D(out2), .Q(out1));
endmodule
EOF
hierarchy -top top
setattr -set init 8'h00 top/w:out1
select -clear
check -assert
design -save orig

opt_retime -flop f1 -cut k_not -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved false
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.refusal "Net \out2 drives an output of top as well as flop f1, so moving the register across the u boundary would change what that output is worth on every cycle (unsupported)."
scratchpad -assert-unset opt.did_something

design -load orig
flatten
hierarchy -top top
design -save goldflat

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u
select -clear
opt_retime -flop f1 -cut u.k_not -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
check -assert
select -assert-count 1 t:$dff
# out2 keeps a combinational copy of its own, which is how the flat move gets
# away with it
select -assert-count 1 top/c:u.k_not_dup %x:+[Y] top/w:u.z %i
flatten
hierarchy -top top
rename top gate
design -import goldflat -as gold
miter -equiv -flatten -make_assert gold gate miter
hierarchy -top miter
proc; opt
sat -seq 8 -prove-asserts -verify
design -reset
log -pop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (b) A register is its clock as much as it is its D and Q, and noclk has no
# clock net for the resolver to find. Flattening makes the question disappear
# rather than answering it: there is one module afterwards and it is top, which
# has the clock.
#
# So the oracle is right that the retiming exists and wrong about how to have
# it. Refusing is correct; the number the oracle puts on it is the size of what
# a logic-moving pass, or an LLM told to prefer that form, would be worth here.
#
# The RTL that expresses it is smaller than "bring the adder out of noclk",
# which is what the flat netlist looks like it is asking for. The register pair
# can sit in front of the instance instead of behind it and noclk does not
# change at all: reg(a) + reg(b) is reg(a + b) for any function of a and b that
# the module happens to compute. That form is written out and proved at the end
# of this section, because a refusal whose stated reason is "the register
# cannot be clocked there" is worth pairing with the move where the register
# never goes there.
log -header "Differ: the destination has no clock"
log -push
design -reset
read_verilog -icells <<EOF
module noclk (a, b, y);
  input [7:0] a, b;
  output [7:0] y;
  $add #(.A_WIDTH(8), .B_WIDTH(8), .Y_WIDTH(8), .A_SIGNED(0), .B_SIGNED(0))
    n_add (.A(a), .B(b), .Y(y));
endmodule
module top (clk, a, b, out);
  input clk;
  input [7:0] a, b;
  output [7:0] out;
  wire [7:0] mid;
  noclk u (.a(a), .b(b), .y(mid));
  $dff #(.WIDTH(8), .CLK_POLARITY(1'b1)) f1 (.CLK(clk), .D(mid), .Q(out));
endmodule
EOF
hierarchy -top top
setattr -set init 8'h00 top/w:out
select -clear
check -assert
design -save orig

opt_retime -flop f1 -cut n_add -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved false
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.refusal "Flop f1 takes its clock from \clk, which is not a net of noclk, so the register cannot be clocked there."
scratchpad -assert-unset opt.did_something
# no marker: this is the answer being no rather than not yet
select -assert-count 0 noclk/t:$dff

design -load orig
flatten
hierarchy -top top
design -save goldflat

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u
select -clear
opt_retime -flop f1 -cut u.n_add -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
check -assert
select -assert-count 2 t:$dff
flatten
hierarchy -top top
rename top gate
design -import goldflat -as gold
miter -equiv -flatten -make_assert gold gate miter
hierarchy -top miter
proc; opt
sat -seq 8 -prove-asserts -verify
design -reset

# The hierarchical form of the same retiming. noclk is the module it was, byte
# for byte, and the two registers the oracle's move produced are in top in front
# of the instance rather than inside it. Nothing is clocked anywhere new and no
# logic changed modules, so the destination never needed the clock the refusal
# is about.
read_verilog -icells <<EOF
module noclk (a, b, y);
  input [7:0] a, b;
  output [7:0] y;
  $add #(.A_WIDTH(8), .B_WIDTH(8), .Y_WIDTH(8), .A_SIGNED(0), .B_SIGNED(0))
    n_add (.A(a), .B(b), .Y(y));
endmodule
module top (clk, a, b, out);
  input clk;
  input [7:0] a, b;
  output [7:0] out;
  (* init = 8'h00 *) wire [7:0] qa;
  (* init = 8'h00 *) wire [7:0] qb;
  $dff #(.WIDTH(8), .CLK_POLARITY(1'b1)) fa (.CLK(clk), .D(a), .Q(qa));
  $dff #(.WIDTH(8), .CLK_POLARITY(1'b1)) fb (.CLK(clk), .D(b), .Q(qb));
  noclk u (.a(qa), .b(qb), .y(out));
endmodule
EOF
hierarchy -top top
check -assert
select -assert-count 0 noclk/t:$dff
select -assert-count 2 top/t:$dff
select -assert-count 1 noclk/c:n_add %x:+[Y] noclk/w:y %i
flatten
hierarchy -top top
rename top gate
design -import goldflat -as gold
miter -equiv -flatten -make_assert gold gate miter
hierarchy -top miter
proc; opt
sat -seq 8 -prove-asserts -verify
design -reset
log -pop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (b) The case the clock check was written for, and the one where relocating
# really is impossible: \gated is made by an $icg inside gater, so there is no
# net in top carrying it and nothing for the resolver to find however far it
# looks. Punching a clock port would be building a clock tree and generating the
# gate again in top would be duplicating it; neither is retiming.
#
# The oracle gets the clock for free by deleting the boundary that was keeping
# it out, and the register it produces sits in the one remaining module reading
# \u.gated. That is the disagreement stated exactly: the move needs the gated
# clock wherever the register is, and only one of the two paths has to care.
#
# The form that does exist brings the xor into gater and behind fg, which never
# moves and keeps the clock it always had - the same "move logic, not state"
# answer as the section above, on the refusal where it is the only answer.
#
# Checked structurally rather than by SAT, following opt_retime_clockgate.ys:29
# - $icg has no SAT model, so a miter holding one cannot be solved. The section
# above is the same claim about the same refusal with the gating taken out, and
# it is proved there.
log -header "Differ: the clock is gated inside the child"
log -push
design -reset
read_verilog -icells <<EOF
module gater (clk, en, x, y);
  input clk, en;
  input [7:0] x;
  output [7:0] y;
  wire gated;
  $icg g0 (.CLK(clk), .EN(en), .SE(1'b0), .GCLK(gated));
  $dff #(.WIDTH(8), .CLK_POLARITY(1'b1)) fg (.CLK(gated), .D(x), .Q(y));
endmodule
module top (clk, en, a, out);
  input clk, en;
  input [7:0] a;
  output [7:0] out;
  wire [7:0] pre;
  $xor #(.A_WIDTH(8), .B_WIDTH(8), .Y_WIDTH(8), .A_SIGNED(0), .B_SIGNED(0))
    t_xor (.A(a), .B(8'h0f), .Y(pre));
  gater u (.clk(clk), .en(en), .x(pre), .y(out));
endmodule
EOF
hierarchy -top top
setattr -set init 8'h00 gater/w:y
select -clear
check -assert
design -save orig

scratchpad -unset opt.did_something
opt_retime -flop fg -cut t_xor -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved false
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.refusal "Flop fg takes its clock from \gated, which is not a net of top, so the register cannot be clocked there."
scratchpad -assert-unset opt.did_something

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u
select -clear
opt_retime -flop u.fg -cut t_xor -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
check -assert
select -assert-count 1 top/t:$dff
select -assert-count 1 top/t:$icg
# the register reads top's input and the xor is behind it, and it is clocked by
# the net the $icg makes, which after the flatten is a net of the only module
select -assert-count 1 top/c:u.fg %x:+[D] top/w:a %i
select -assert-count 1 top/c:u.fg %x:+[CLK] top/w:u.gated %i
select -assert-count 1 top/c:u.g0 %x:+[GCLK] top/w:u.gated %i
design -reset

# The form that keeps the hierarchy: fg still reads gater's input port under
# \gated, the xor that used to be top's is behind it, and top holds the instance
# and nothing else.
read_verilog -icells <<EOF
module gater (clk, en, x, y);
  input clk, en;
  input [7:0] x;
  output [7:0] y;
  wire gated;
  (* init = 8'h0f *) wire [7:0] xq;
  $icg g0 (.CLK(clk), .EN(en), .SE(1'b0), .GCLK(gated));
  $dff #(.WIDTH(8), .CLK_POLARITY(1'b1)) fg (.CLK(gated), .D(x), .Q(xq));
  $xor #(.A_WIDTH(8), .B_WIDTH(8), .Y_WIDTH(8), .A_SIGNED(0), .B_SIGNED(0))
    g_xor (.A(xq), .B(8'h0f), .Y(y));
endmodule
module top (clk, en, a, out);
  input clk, en;
  input [7:0] a;
  output [7:0] out;
  gater u (.clk(clk), .en(en), .x(a), .y(out));
endmodule
EOF
hierarchy -top top
check -assert
select -assert-count 1 gater/c:fg %x:+[CLK] gater/w:gated %i
select -assert-count 1 gater/c:fg %x:+[D] gater/w:x %i
select -assert-count 1 gater/c:g_xor %x:+[A] gater/w:xq %i
select -assert-count 0 top/t:$xor
select -assert-count 0 top/t:$dff
select -assert-count 1 top/c:u %x:+[x] top/w:a %i
design -reset
log -pop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (b) The last refusal, and the one where the oracle's success is a technicality
# worth reading carefully.
#
# FfData::emit writes the register's init onto its Q net, and FfInitVals
# log_errors on a sigmap group holding two different init values, so a landing
# net that already carries one is a crash a command later rather than a wrong
# answer now. The pass therefore checks before it touches anything, and refuses.
#
# The oracle moves. It can, because the init the refusal is protecting sits on a
# net the adder drives combinationally, where an initial value is inert - proc
# says so out loud on the gold side, and the warning is asserted below. So the
# flat pass overwrites a value that never meant anything and the result is
# equivalent to the original.
#
# The refusal is still load-bearing: without it the crash is real. But it is a
# check about a net that has an attribute, not about whether the move exists,
# and on this design the move does - so it wants the marker that says so rather
# than no marker at all.
log -header "Differ: an inert init on the landing net"
log -push
design -reset
read_verilog -icells opt_retime_hier.v
hierarchy -top top
setattr -set init 8'h55 hier_src/w:y
select -clear
check -assert
design -save orig
flatten
hierarchy -top top
design -save goldflat

design -load orig
scratchpad -unset opt.did_something
opt_retime -flop f1 -cut add_s -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved false
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.refusal "Net \y in hier_src starts at 8'01010101 and flop f1 would land on it starting at 8'00000000, and a net cannot hold two initial values."
scratchpad -assert-unset opt.did_something
select -assert-count 1 hier_src/w:y a:init=8'h55 %i
select -assert-count 0 hier_src/t:$dff

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u_src
select -clear
opt_retime -flop f1 -cut u_src.add_s -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
check -assert
# the 8'h55 is gone, replaced by the folded init of the register that landed
select -assert-count 1 top/w:u_src.y a:init=8'h00 %i
select -assert-count 2 top/t:$dff
flatten
hierarchy -top top
rename top gate
design -import goldflat -as gold
miter -equiv -flatten -make_assert gold gate miter
hierarchy -top miter
proc
# The gold side says out loud that the 8'h55 never meant anything: proc_init
# drops an init on a wire no register drives. That is the argument of this
# section, so it is asserted rather than tolerated - if the attribute ever does
# become load-bearing here, this line is what notices.
logger -expect warning "ignoring initial value on non-register: .gold\.u_src\.y" 1
opt
sat -seq 8 -prove-asserts -verify
logger -check-expected
design -reset
log -pop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (a) -all-fanouts finds the sibling registers by their shared D net and then
# re-derives the move at each one by replaying the route to the cut as a list of
# port names, which is a walk inside one module. Flattened, every route is
# inside one module by construction, so the oracle widens the move without
# noticing there was anything to notice.
log -header "Differ: -all-fanouts across a boundary"
log -push
design -reset
read_verilog -icells opt_retime_hier.v
hierarchy -top top
check -assert
design -save orig

opt_retime -flop f1 -cut add_s -backward -all-fanouts
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved false
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.refusal "-all-fanouts replays the route to the cut as port names within one module, so it cannot widen a move that crosses the boundary at add_s (unsupported)."
scratchpad -assert-unset opt.did_something

design -load orig
flatten
hierarchy -top top
design -save goldflat

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u_src
select -clear
opt_retime -flop f1 -cut u_src.add_s -backward -all-fanouts
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
check -assert
select -assert-count 2 t:$dff
flatten
hierarchy -top top
rename top gate
design -import goldflat -as gold
miter -equiv -flatten -make_assert gold gate miter
hierarchy -top miter
proc; opt
sat -seq 8 -prove-asserts -verify
design -reset
log -pop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (c) The disagreement that runs the other way. leaf has two instance sites; the
# pass refuses because a move edits a module body and there is one body for both
# of them, and the oracle inlines u1, retimes inside it, and produces a correct
# flat netlist that no edit to leaf.v expresses.
#
# opt_retime_hier_oracle.ys's last section proves that netlist and walks through
# why it cannot be mirrored. Repeated here only as the pair of verdicts, because
# a differential file that recorded the other four disagreements and not this
# one would read as though the oracle were the authority.
log -header "Differ: two instance sites, and here the oracle is the wrong one"
log -push
design -reset
read_verilog -icells <<EOF
module leaf (clk, x, z);
  input clk;
  input [7:0] x;
  output [7:0] z;
  $not #(.A_WIDTH(8), .Y_WIDTH(8), .A_SIGNED(0)) l_not (.A(x), .Y(z));
endmodule
module top (clk, a, b, out1, out2);
  input clk;
  input [7:0] a, b;
  output [7:0] out1, out2;
  wire [7:0] q1, q2;
  $dff #(.WIDTH(8), .CLK_POLARITY(1'b1)) f1 (.CLK(clk), .D(a), .Q(q1));
  $dff #(.WIDTH(8), .CLK_POLARITY(1'b1)) f2 (.CLK(clk), .D(b), .Q(q2));
  leaf u1 (.clk(clk), .x(q1), .z(out1));
  leaf u2 (.clk(clk), .x(q2), .z(out2));
endmodule
EOF
hierarchy -top top
check -assert
design -save orig

opt_retime -flop f1 -cut l_not -forward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved false
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.refusal "Module leaf has 2 instance sites and opt_retime would be moving a register at every one of them, having only the one body to edit. Uniquify first (unsupported)."
scratchpad -assert-unset opt.did_something

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u1
select -clear
opt_retime -flop f1 -cut u1.l_not -forward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
check -assert
# u1's copy is retimed and u2's is not, and leaf itself is untouched: one body,
# two behaviours, which is the shape that has no RTL
select -assert-count 1 top/c:u1.l_not %x:+[A] top/w:a %i
select -assert-count 1 leaf/c:l_not %x:+[A] leaf/w:x %i
select -assert-count 1 top/c:u2 %x:+[x] top/w:q2 %i
design -reset
log -pop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Part 3: where the oracle has nothing to say.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A blackbox has no body, so flatten leaves the instance standing and there is
# no cell inside it to name as a cut. Both paths decline, for once, and for the
# same reason - the difference being that the pass says so and the oracle simply
# has no way to be asked.
log -header "Agree by inability: the boundary module is a blackbox"
log -push
design -reset
read_verilog -icells <<EOF
(* blackbox *)
module bb (clk, x, z);
  input clk;
  input [7:0] x;
  output [7:0] z;
endmodule
module top (clk, a, out);
  input clk;
  input [7:0] a;
  output [7:0] out;
  wire [7:0] q;
  $dff #(.WIDTH(8), .CLK_POLARITY(1'b1)) f1 (.CLK(clk), .D(a), .Q(q));
  bb u (.clk(clk), .x(q), .z(out));
endmodule
EOF
hierarchy -top top
design -save orig

opt_retime -flop f1 -cut u -forward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved false
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.refusal "Module bb is a blackbox, so it has no body for flop f1 to move into."

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u
select -clear
# the instance is still there afterwards, so the oracle's scope never grew and
# there is still nothing to cut at
select -assert-count 1 top/c:u
select -assert-count 2 top/c:*
design -reset
log -pop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The pure crossing: cut is the instance, so the move relocates the register
# across the boundary and touches no logic at all. It is the primitive every
# section in Part 1 is built out of, and it is the one request the oracle cannot
# be asked, because flattening deletes the boundary and the move with it. There
# is no cell named u_src afterwards and nothing that stands for it.
#
# Not a refusal on either side. Natively it is an ordinary success - the first
# section of opt_retime_hier.ys - and against the oracle it is a malformed
# request, which is the honest answer: the cut names a cell that does not exist.
#
# logger -expect error exits yosys successfully, so this has to come last.
log -header "No oracle: a crossing with no logic in it"
log -push
design -reset
read_verilog -icells opt_retime_hier.v
hierarchy -top top
check -assert
design -save orig

opt_retime -flop f1 -cut u_src -backward
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved true
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_from top
scratchpad -assert opt_retime.moved_to hier_src
check -assert

design -load orig
flatten -noscopeinfo top/c:u_src
select -clear
select -assert-count 0 top/c:u_src
logger -expect error "Cut cell 'u_src' not found in module top or in any other selected module." 1
opt_retime -flop f1 -cut u_src -backward
